          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My knees hurt most days and I have been told to lose weight. Is joint pain ever urgent, and what does guidance describe as core treatment?",
          "answer": "Triage did not flag an emergency from what you described. That is not the same as saying nothing is serious, so please seek medical advice if your knee becomes severe, worsens, or changes in a way that worries you.\n\n**When joint pain needs prompt assessment.** The osteoarthritis summary (condition-osteoarthritis) says a joint that is hot, swollen, very painful, and comes with fever needs urgent assessment, because it may signal infection or crystal arthritis rather than ordinary wear-and-tear pain. Sudden locking or giving way of a joint also warrants prompt assessment. The same summary notes that long morning stiffness, several small joints affected evenly, or whole-body symptoms point toward inflammatory arthritis, which is worth raising with a clinician.\n\n**What guidance describes as core treatment.** The osteoarthritis guideline (guideline-osteoarthritis-management) says non-drug management comes first and forms the foundation of treatment, not an optional extra. For knee osteoarthritis, structured exercise has the strongest evidence of any option, with benefit comparable to medicines and a much better safety profile. The programme combines strengthening the muscles around the knee, keeping the joint moving through its range, and aerobic activity. The guideline notes this works regardless of how worn the joint looks on imaging, and that some discomfort during exercise is not in itself a sign of damage. Guidance also recommends starting with supervision, since technique and progression matter. The lifestyle note (lifestyle-osteoarthritis-activity) describes the same three components in more detail. The condition summary adds that weight management, education, pacing, suitable footwear, and heat or cold can help, and that medicines are add-ons. Joint replacement is considered only when pain and limitation persist despite good non-surgical care.\n\n**What these sources did not cover.** The retrieved passages do not give details of weight-loss programmes or specific exercises for your situation, and they do not tell you which treatments suit you. A clinician or physiotherapist can assess your knee and help plan an exercise programme and any next steps.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
